@host = http://localhost:3001

### === §7.7 Report & Moderation ===
### Seeded test users: 1 = regular user, 2 = admin A, 3 = admin B

### 1. POST /reports — no auth header -> expect 401
POST {{host}}/reports
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "report_type": "Post",
  "target_id": 1,
  "reason_category": "Fake item"
}

### 2. POST /reports — with x-user-id -> expect 201 + status: Pending
# @name createReport1
POST {{host}}/reports
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 1

{
  "report_type": "Post",
  "target_id": 1,
  "reason_category": "Fake item",
  "details": "Item photos don't match description",
  "evidence_urls": ["https://example.com/evidence1.jpg"]
}

### 3. POST /reports — invalid report_type -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/reports
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 1

{
  "report_type": "NotARealType",
  "target_id": 1,
  "reason_category": "Fake item"
}

### 4. GET /admin/reports — no admin header -> expect 403
GET {{host}}/admin/reports
x-user-id: 1

### 5. GET /admin/reports — as admin -> expect 200 + list
GET {{host}}/admin/reports
x-user-id: 2
x-is-admin: true

### 6. POST /admin/reports/:id/resolve — as admin A -> expect 200, status: Violation_Confirmed
### uses the report created in #2 — run #2 first
POST {{host}}/admin/reports/{{createReport1.response.body.$.report_id}}/resolve
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 2
x-is-admin: true

{
  "status": "Violation_Confirmed",
  "resolution_note": "Confirmed after reviewing chat history."
}

### 7. POST /admin/reports/999/resolve — nonexistent report -> expect 404
POST {{host}}/admin/reports/999/resolve
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 2
x-is-admin: true

{
  "status": "Violation_Confirmed"
}

### 8. POST /reports — create a 2nd report to test /restore on
# @name createReport2
POST {{host}}/reports
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 1

{
  "report_type": "Post",
  "target_id": 2,
  "reason_category": "Reported unfairly"
}

### 9. POST /admin/reports/:id/restore — as admin A -> expect 200, status: Report_Dismissed
### uses the report created in #8 — run #8 first
POST {{host}}/admin/reports/{{createReport2.response.body.$.report_id}}/restore
x-user-id: 2
x-is-admin: true

### === Appeals ===

### 10. POST /appeals — appeal report from #2 (already resolved) -> expect 201
# @name createAppeal1
POST {{host}}/appeals
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 1

{
  "report_id": {{createReport1.response.body.$.report_id}},
  "reason": "I believe this decision was unfair, item was as described.",
  "evidence_urls": ["https://example.com/appeal-evidence.jpg"]
}

### 11. POST /appeals — appeal report from #8 (already restored/dismissed) -> expect 201
POST {{host}}/appeals
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 1

{
  "report_id": {{createReport2.response.body.$.report_id}},
  "reason": "Testing appeal on already-restored report"
}

### 12. POST /admin/appeals/:id/decide — same admin who resolved report 1 -> expect 400
### uses the appeal created in #10 — run #10 first
POST {{host}}/admin/appeals/{{createAppeal1.response.body.$.appeal_id}}/decide
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 2
x-is-admin: true

{
  "status": "Approved"
}

### 13. POST /admin/appeals/:id/decide — different admin (admin B) -> expect 200
POST {{host}}/admin/appeals/{{createAppeal1.response.body.$.appeal_id}}/decide
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 3
x-is-admin: true

{
  "status": "Approved",
  "resolution_note": "Reviewed chat log, agree the item matched description."
}

### === Appeal ownership fix — Post/Transaction target_id != user_id ===
### mock owner map (src/common/mock-data/mock-target-owners.ts): Post:10 -> user 1

### 14. POST /reports — report a Post whose real owner is user 1, but post_id (10) != owner's user_id
# @name createPostReport
POST {{host}}/reports
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 2

{
  "report_type": "Post",
  "target_id": 10,
  "reason_category": "Fake item"
}

### 15. POST /admin/reports/:id/resolve — as admin A -> expect 200, status: Violation_Confirmed
### run #14 first (Send Request) so {{createPostReport.response.body.$.report_id}} resolves
POST {{host}}/admin/reports/{{createPostReport.response.body.$.report_id}}/resolve
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 2
x-is-admin: true

{
  "status": "Violation_Confirmed"
}

### 16. POST /appeals — wrong user (id 10, matches the post_id, NOT the real owner) -> expect 403
### Before the fix this would have wrongly PASSED because target_id (10) was compared
### directly against appellantId. Now it should be blocked with 403.
POST {{host}}/appeals
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 10

{
  "report_id": {{createPostReport.response.body.$.report_id}},
  "reason": "I own this post (I don't, my user_id just matches the post_id)"
}

### 17. POST /appeals — real owner (user 1) appeals -> expect 201
### Before the fix this would have wrongly gotten 403 because target_id (10) != appellantId (1).
POST {{host}}/appeals
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 1

{
  "report_id": {{createPostReport.response.body.$.report_id}},
  "reason": "This is my post, the report is wrong"
}

### === Double-decide guard — an already-decided appeal must not be overwritten ===

### 18. POST /admin/appeals/:id/decide — decide the appeal from #13 a SECOND time -> expect 400
### #13 already approved this appeal (which set its report to Report_Dismissed).
### Before the fix there was no status check, so this Rejected would have gone through:
### appeal would end up Rejected while its report stayed Report_Dismissed — contradictory,
### and handled_by/handled_at of the original decision would be silently overwritten.
POST {{host}}/admin/appeals/{{createAppeal1.response.body.$.appeal_id}}/decide
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 1
x-is-admin: true

{
  "status": "Rejected",
  "resolution_note": "Attempt to overwrite an already-approved appeal"
}
